[b]Structural Wave Field[/b] is a chart-native market-structure visualization. Instead of a classical oscillator, it converts structural flow, internal wave energy, correction pressure, maturity, re-synchronization and exhaustion into an adaptive gradient field on a shared Flow Axis below price.

[b]What it does[/b]

A multi-horizon Structural Flow Engine (fast/medium/slow, with hysteresis) establishes the active structural direction and holds it through ordinary corrections. A separate, faster Internal Wave Engine (momentum/velocity/acceleration) reads the wave currently running inside that structural leg — aligned with structure, or counter to it. The two are fused into a gradient field so you see structural direction, internal wave strength and counter pressure at a glance, without reading a separate panel.

[b]Core engines[/b]

[list]
[*][b]Structural Flow[/b] — multi-horizon displacement, efficiency, persistence and alignment with separate hold/entry/flip thresholds
[*][b]Internal Wave[/b] — momentum/velocity/acceleration fused into one bounded wave read
[*][b]Wave Segmentation[/b] — non-overlapping aligned/counter segments with duration, directional displacement, peak, integral, quality, efficiency, participation and retracement memory
[*][b]Structural-Leg Archive[/b] — normalizes maturity/extension against the instrument's own recent leg history
[*][b]Counter Pressure / Structural Integrity[/b] — how much of the active leg is being eroded
[*][b]Maturity, Compression, Re-Sync, Exhaustion, Failed Expansion[/b] — Re-Sync owns and consumes one completed counter segment, then applies duration/depth/quality/structure gates and adaptive cooldown
[*][b]Elliott-Context[/b] — quality-gates non-overlapping five-segment impulse and three-segment correction windows without asserting a hard wave count
[*][b]Adaptive sensitivity[/b] — Fast, Balanced and Structural presets plus opt-in advanced calibration; invalid threshold/horizon relationships are rejected explicitly
[/list]

[b]Phase Engine[/b]

All of the above resolves into one of nine persistent phases — Initiation, Acceleration, Expansion, Correction, Compression, Re-Sync, Late Expansion, Exhaustion, Structural Flow Flip — each with an approximate confidence read, shown via compact hover labels and a dashboard. Ordinary changes require short persistence to reduce one-bar phase flicker. Flow Flip means the multi-horizon flow model reversed; it is not presented as a pivot-based BOS/CHoCH.

[b]Visual field[/b]

The field follows a short rolling price envelope at an ATR-normalized clearance and participates in chart price scaling. Its thin neutral anchor communicates placement only and disappears outside active structure or transition memory; direction and state come from the colored backbone and body. Aligned energy grows above it, optional counter pressure below it. A continuous gradient fades toward a subtle contour, while early deterioration ambers the edge before full Exhaustion. After flow exits into neutral, the completed leg remains for a sensitivity-scaled fading transition memory, including its last maturity, exhaustion and wave context in the dashboard. Directional RS↑/RS↓ labels consume one completed counter segment; quality-gated I5/C3 labels own non-overlapping segment windows. The chart candles are never colored or overdrawn.

[b]Realtime behavior[/b]

The field and dashboard update live. Named event labels and alerts wait for bar close by default, preventing transient intrabar events from appearing and disappearing; users can explicitly enable live events.

